                <head>134. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>At the 380th meeting of the National Security Council on September 25, Deputy
                    Director of Central Intelligence C. P. Cabell briefed the Council on significant
                    world developments affecting U.S. security. Cabell briefly referred to Cuba,
                    noting that Castro’s rebel movement “was in a stronger position than ever
                    before.” There was no other discussion of the Cuban situation. (Memorandum of
                    discussion; Eisenhower Library, Whitman File, NSC Records)</p>
            </div>
